  "query_preview": "..(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 834 AM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nExpect a significant drop in temperatures to occur on Friday \nevening, with temperatures in the 50s area-wide by sunset. Once \nthe sun goes down and solar insolation goes away, the temperature \nplummet will continue. The entire CWA should wake up to \ntemperatures in the 40s on Saturday morning, but lingering cloud \ncover and some upsloped rain showers should prevent it from \ngetting much colder than that. \n\nThe same cannot be said for Saturday night/Sunday morning, which \nlooks to be the coldest night of the autumn season thus far. As a \npost-frontal surface high pressure system builds into the CWA on \nSaturday morning, winds will shift to a more northerly orientation. \nThis will bring an end to any orographically-enhanced post-frontal \nrain chances, and a much cooler/drier continental airmass will move \ninto the region in the wake of the precipitation. Highs will \nstruggle to get much warmer than the 50s, and dewpoints will plummet \ninto the 30s on Saturday afternoon. Diurnal mixing should work to \nerode any lingering cloud cover by sunset, which should set the \nstage for efficient radiational cooling overnight. NBM ensemble data \nresolves a 60-90% chance of temperatures dropping below 36 degrees \nacross most of the JKL CWA overnight. The most sheltered and shaded \nvalleys could make a run at the 32 degree mark, but the probability \nof occurrence drops to below 40% at this threshold. As a reminder, \nthe criteria for a Frost Advisory is defined as minimum shelter \nforecast temperatures between 33 and 36 degrees Fahrenheit during \nthe growing season, on nights with good radiational cooling \nconditions. Saturday night is poised to meet this criteria, so \nagricultural interests and recreational gardeners are encouraged to \nmonitor for potential product issuances as the time approaches.  \n  \nTemperatures will be slow to make a full recovery back above the 70 \ndegree mark next week, although a gradual warm-up is expected. The \npostfrontal high should shift east on Sunday as the mid/upper level \ntrough axis aloft swings through New England. Antecedent forecast \nthinking is that a building ridge will allow temperatures to \nmoderate back into the 60s under fair skies by early next week. \nHowever, some of the more recent pieces of guidance (namely members \nof the Euro ensemble) are developing a cut-off low over the \nCarolinas in this time frame. That feature could block the eastward \npropagation of that ridging, and it would cause temperatures to stay \non the lower side of the forecast envelope early next week. \nNevertheless, fog should become favored over frost in the valleys at \nnight as temperatures warm. This is typical for autumn in Eastern \nKentucky, and next week's forecast features temperatures much closer \nto normal than what we are experiencing at the present moment.",
